steps.body:
`nullptr` is the null pointer: a pointer that points at nothing. It makes a good "not found yet" value. Before you dereference a pointer that might be null, check it:

```cpp
int* found = nullptr;
if (found != nullptr) {   // or simply: if (found)
    std::cout << *found << '\n';
}
```

Dereferencing `nullptr` is undefined behavior. On this machine it usually crashes the program. A crash also throws away any output still waiting in `std::cout`'s buffer, so lines you printed just before it may never appear. Missing output does not mean the crash happened earlier.

To point at an array element while scanning, loop by reference with `for (int& x : nums)`, so `&x` is the address of the real element. With `for (int x : nums)`, `x` is a copy that disappears each iteration, and a pointer to it dangles. A pointer to an element also lets you change that element in the array.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    int nums[5] = {7, 3, 10, 5, 8};

    int* firstEven = nullptr;
    for (int& x : nums) {
        if (x % 2 == 0) {
            firstEven = &x;
            break;
        }
    }
    if (firstEven != nullptr) {
        std::cout << *firstEven << '\n';
        *firstEven = 0;
        std::cout << nums[2] << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << "none\n";
    }

    int* firstNegative = nullptr;
    for (int& x : nums) {
        if (x < 0) {
            firstNegative = &x;
            break;
        }
    }
    if (firstNegative) {
        std::cout << *firstNegative << '\n';
    } else {
        std::cout << "none\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

