#include <iostream>

void resetValue(int n) {
    n = 0;
}

void resetRef(int& n) {
    n = 0;
}

void resetPtr(int* p) {
    if (p == nullptr) {
        return;
    }
    *p = 0;
}

int readOr(const int* p, int fallback) {
    if (p) {
        return *p;
    }
    return fallback;
}

int main() {
    int lives = 3;
    int coins = 7;
    int gems = 4;

    resetValue(lives);
    std::cout << lives << ' ';
    resetRef(coins);
    std::cout << coins << ' ';
    resetPtr(&gems);
    std::cout << gems << ' ';
    resetPtr(nullptr);
    std::cout << gems << '\n';

    std::cout << readOr(&lives, -1) << ' ' << readOr(nullptr, -1) << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
An array name *decays* to a pointer to its first element, so `int* p = data` works with no `&`. Pointer arithmetic moves by whole elements: `p + 1` is the next `int`, `sizeof(int)` (4) bytes further on, and on a `double*` it would be 8 bytes. `*(p + i)` is exactly `p[i]`. To walk an array, go from `data` to one past the end, `data + n`, which you may compare against but never dereference.

```cpp
for (const int* p = data; p != data + 5; ++p) {
    std::cout << *p << '\n';
}
```

A function parameter that looks like an array, such as `const int arr[]`, is really a pointer, so the size is lost (`sizeof` inside gives 8). Pass the count too: `int sum(const int* arr, int n)`.

A string literal such as `"memory"` is a `const char` array with a hidden `'\0'` at the end. That terminator is how code finds the end: walk until `*c == '\0'`.

`std::cout << c` on a `char*` prints text from `c` up to the next `'\0'`, not one character. So print `*c` when walking, and never print a `char` array that lacks a `'\0'`, because output would run past its end.

example.code:
#include <iostream>

int sum(const int* arr, int n) {
    int total = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        total += arr[i];
    }
    return total;
}

int main() {
    int data[5] = {2, 4, 6, 8, 10};
    for (const int* p = data; p != data + 5; ++p) {
        if (p != data) {
            std::cout << ' ';
        }
        std::cout << *p;
    }
    std::cout << '\n';

    const int* first = data;
    std::cout << *(first + 2) << ' ' << first[2] << '\n';
    std::cout << sum(data, 5) << '\n';

    const char* word = "memory";
    int length = 0;
    for (const char* c = word; *c != '\0'; ++c) {
        ++length;
    }
    std::cout << word << ' ' << (word + 3) << ' ' << length << '\n';
    return 0;
}

